1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,278
In 1878, Lake Michigan took a lighthouse

2
00:00:03,288 --> 00:00:05,299
keeper, his wife and their baby.

3
00:00:05,529 --> 00:00:07,369
One of their sons later helped keep

4
00:00:07,379 --> 00:00:08,787
Michigan City's pier light.

5
00:00:09,337 --> 00:00:11,576
Aaron Sheridan kept the South Manitou

6
00:00:11,586 --> 00:00:12,271
Island light.

7
00:00:12,501 --> 00:00:15,034
His wife, Julia, was his assistant keeper.

8
00:00:15,484 --> 00:00:19,267
On March 15, 1878, they were coming home

9
00:00:19,277 --> 00:00:20,987
from the mainland in a small boat with

10
00:00:20,997 --> 00:00:22,665
their son Robert and a fisherman.

11
00:00:23,215 --> 00:00:25,610
Within a mile of the island, it overturned

12
00:00:25,620 --> 00:00:26,588
in icy water.

13
00:00:26,818 --> 00:00:28,209
The fisherman was rescued.

14
00:00:28,439 --> 00:00:29,646
The Sheridans drowned.

15
00:00:30,496 --> 00:00:32,106
The lake never gave them back.

16
00:00:32,336 --> 00:00:34,054
Their five other sons were sent to

17
00:00:34,064 --> 00:00:35,445
grandparents in Illinois.

18
00:00:36,095 --> 00:00:37,988
One of them, George, grew up to keep

19
00:00:37,998 --> 00:00:38,494
lights too.

20
00:00:38,724 --> 00:00:41,983
In 1905, he became first assistant keeper

21
00:00:41,993 --> 00:00:43,839
of Michigan City's new pier light.

22
00:00:44,389 --> 00:00:46,426
Another Sheridan ran that light until

23
00:00:46,436 --> 00:00:47,322
1930.

24
00:00:48,072 --> 00:00:50,115
Comment your town, and it could be next.

